{
  "rationale": "Evidence: the baseline (iteration 0) went off track on every trial of all three tracks. Progress was only 0.125 on the oval, 0.04 on the s-bend and 0.035 on the slalom, so the robot left the line within roughly 0.1 to 0.4 s of starting. Max error reached about 0.06 m, which is the off-track limit, and line_loss_events was 1.0 on every trial. Steering oscillation was low (about 0.07), so the failure is a large overshoot rather than jitter. Diagnosis: kp = 5 with sensor weights of +/-1 gives a turn of up to 5 per unit of signal, about 1750 PWM of differential. That is too aggressive for a sensor array whose signal jumps as tape moves between footprints. Once the robot overshoots, it loses the line and the last_direction search drives it further out. Change: reduce kp from 5.0 to 3.0 to soften the response, and raise kd from 0.1 to 0.3 to damp the overshoot on each correction. Geometry and base_speed are held fixed so this race isolates the gain effect. Expected result: the robot stays within about 0.04 m of the line, so progress rises well above 0.1 on the oval, line_loss_events falls toward 0, and off-track endings become rarer. Completion is not yet expected. Hypotheses: (1) over-steering is the main cause, so lower kp keeps the robot on the tape; (2) if progress still stalls near 0.1 with max_error near 0.06, the sensor spread of x = +/-0.04 is too narrow to hold the line through bends, and the next revision should widen it to x = +/-0.06 or raise sensor_size to 0.016. Measurements wanted next: per-track progress, max_error, line_loss_events and steering_oscillation, to confirm whether the failure is overshoot or line loss.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": -0.04, "y": 0.04},
      {"x": -0.02, "y": 0.04},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.04},
      {"x": 0.02, "y": 0.04},
      {"x": 0.04, "y": 0.04}
    ],
